From 519e41698a172add8aa7b54ab4e94daad0af213e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andi Albrecht <albrecht.andi@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 14:46:22 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Pair comment/dollar-quote delimiters at the lexer position

The ReDoS fix for GHSA-prg7-hcfm-mfcr resolved all delimiter pairs in a
single pass over the raw text, before lexing. That pass has no notion of
which offsets are inside a string literal or behind a "--" comment, so a
"/*", "*/" or "$$" occurring there was paired as if it were a real
delimiter -- consuming the partner of the next actual one. Everything
after it was then mistokenized:

    SELECT '/*' FROM t; /* c */ SELECT 1
    -- 0.5.5: Comment.Multiline '/* c */'
    -- broken: Operator '/', Wildcard '*', Name.Builtin 'c', ...

    -- $$
    DO $$ BEGIN NULL; END $$;
    -- 0.5.5: Literal '$$ BEGIN NULL; END $$'
    -- broken: Error '$', Name.Placeholder '$', ...

Collect only the opener and closer positions upfront and pair them with
a binary search when the lexer actually reaches an opener. Delimiters
inside strings or comments are skipped along with their surrounding
token and never resolved, while unmatched openers still cost one failed
lookup each instead of a rescan, so the fix stays linear.

Also match dollar-quote delimiters through a lookahead so overlapping
occurrences are found: in `$$$a$$` the closing `$$` starts inside the
match of the opening one, which the non-overlapping scan missed.

Verified against 0.5.5 over 47900 delimiter-heavy snippets with an
identical token stream, and benchmarks/bench_dollar_quote_redos.py still
scales linearly.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
